# 1. Purpose and Scope

This document defines the application architecture for the OPEX Budgeting System — an internal web-based application supporting budgeting and workforce planning across cost centers. It establishes the technical envelope within which all functional requirements, security controls, and UI specifications must be designed.

This specification is intended to be consumed directly by development teams and AI-assisted code generation tools. All architectural decisions recorded here are binding unless explicitly superseded by a versioned amendment.

## 1.1 System Summary

| **Application type** | **Web application — internet-accessible, restricted to authenticated corporate users via SSO** |
| --- | --- |
| **Primary functions** | Budget planning, workforce planning, actuals reporting, Finance approval workflow |
| **User base** | 50–200 internal users (cost center owners, Finance Reviewers, and executives) |
| **Deployment** | Cloud-hosted on Microsoft Azure |
| **Data platform** | Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server as the primary data store |
| **Integration model** | File-based import (CSV/Excel) from accounting and HR admin systems via Azure Blob Storage |
| **Data refresh** | Hourly (accounting system actuals); daily (HR admin system employees); ad hoc, Administrator-triggered (cost center and account hierarchies) |
| **Authentication** | Microsoft Entra ID via OIDC/OAuth 2.0 (SSO) |

# 2. Architecture Overview

The system follows a three-tier web application architecture: a browser-based single-page application (SPA) frontend, a backend API layer, and a relational data platform. A separate batch processing layer handles file ingestion from source systems on a per-source-system schedule. All business rule evaluation, including calculations, is performed server-side.

## 2.1 Architectural Pattern

- Three-tier: Presentation → API → Data

- Frontend: Single-page application (SPA) served as static assets via Azure CDN / Azure Static Web Apps

- Backend: RESTful API — stateless, horizontally scalable — hosted on Azure App Service (Linux, Python 3.11)

- Data platform: Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server as the single source of truth for all planning and actuals data

- Batch layer: Azure Functions on per-source-system schedules for file ingestion and data processing

- Authentication: Delegated to Microsoft Entra ID via OIDC/OAuth 2.0 — no local credential management

- Calculation model: Reactive server-side calculation service — the frontend never owns authoritative business rule logic

## 2.2 Hosting Model

The backend API is hosted on Azure App Service (Linux, Python 3.11). App Service is the single API hosting track. There is no secondary or fallback hosting track.

| **Claude Code note — hosting-agnostic API** | **The API codebase must be written to be hosting-agnostic — no hosting-platform-specific dependencies in the core API logic. The API serves standard HTTP; only infrastructure configuration (environment variables, connection strings, identity bindings) is environment-specific. This keeps the codebase portable and the local DuckDB development path behaviourally identical to the deployed PostgreSQL path.** |
| --- | --- |

### 2.2.1 Architectural Decision Records — Platform Baseline

The following architectural decisions are the confirmed platform baseline for this specification. They supersede the earlier Databricks-based architecture in full. The migration steps that implement this baseline are defined in the Azure Cloud Migration Specification.

| **ADR** | **Decision** | **Rationale** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ADR-01 | Data platform: Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server. All obs.* tables are PostgreSQL relations within the obs schema, which is the single source of truth. | The Databricks workspace is not available to this project. PostgreSQL Flexible Server provides a managed, highly available relational platform sufficient for the transactional and reporting workload of 50–200 users. |
| ADR-02 | Primary API host: Azure App Service (Linux, Python 3.11). There is no fallback hosting track. | App Service serves the stateless FastAPI application directly over HTTP with managed identity, autoscale, and zone redundancy. |
| ADR-03 | Batch processing: Azure Functions — timer-triggered for actuals (hourly) and employees (daily); Blob Storage event-triggered for hierarchies (ad hoc). | Azure Functions provides scheduled and event-driven execution without a cluster dependency, scaling independently of the interactive API. |
| ADR-04 | Idempotent promotion uses PostgreSQL INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE; audit-log append-only is enforced by a BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE trigger; query performance is served by PostgreSQL indexes. | Native PostgreSQL mechanisms replace Delta Lake MERGE, the delta.appendOnly table property, and Z-ORDER optimisation respectively. |

## 2.3 Design Principles

- The frontend is a display and interaction layer only — it delegates all business rule evaluation to the backend

- Calculation results always originate from the server-side calculation service; the frontend may display optimistic local estimates for simple arithmetic while a server response is in flight, but always reconciles to the server result

- The system never writes back to source systems under any circumstances

- All data mutations are recorded in an immutable audit log

- Business rules are stored as configuration data in PostgreSQL — not hardcoded — so they can be updated without a code deployment

## 2.4 Technology Stack

| **Claude Code note — technology stack** | **The stack below reflects the confirmed decisions: Azure as cloud provider, Azure PostgreSQL Flexible Server as data platform, Microsoft Entra ID as identity provider, and Azure App Service as the API host. React with TypeScript is recommended for the frontend given its mature ecosystem and strong data grid library support suited to planning workflows.** |
| --- | --- |

